# DTS Project Categorisation Scorer

## Purpose
Read the content of a PowerPoint presentation describing a proposed or in-flight
project and objectively score it against the six dimensions defined in Section 6.2
of the DTS Project Delivery Framework (PRINCE2-Aligned), then determine the
resulting governance tier per Section 6.4, applying the dollar gate (6.3) and
severe-risk override rules.

## When to use this skill
- A user provides a PowerPoint (or its extracted text/slide content) about a
  project and asks for a categorisation, governance tier, or risk score.
- A user asks "what tier would this project sit in" or "score this against the
  DTS framework".

## Inputs required
1. The PowerPoint content (slide text, speaker notes, tables — extract all
   available text before scoring).
2. If stated in the deck: estimated project cost and delivery discipline
   (IT Infrastructure / Applications / AI-Automation / Cyber). If absent, note
   this as a gap rather than guessing.

## Scoring method

Score each dimension 1 (Low) to 4 (High) using the rubric below, drawn directly
from Section 6.2 of the framework. For each dimension:
- Extract the specific evidence from the deck that supports the score (a slide
  title, a stated fact, a stakeholder list, a technology named, etc.).
- If the deck is silent on a dimension, default conservatively to the lowest
  defensible score and flag it explicitly as "not addressed in deck" rather
  than inferring detail that isn't there.

### Rubric (from Section 6.2)

| Dimension | 1 — Low | 2 | 3 | 4 — High |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Execution risk | Routine; low chance of failure | Some delivery risk; manageable | Significant risk; active management needed | High risk of failure without intensive control |
| Complexity | Single component; one team/site | Few components; limited integration | Multiple systems, teams or sites integrated | Enterprise-wide; many integration points |
| Familiarity & repeatability | Done often; fully templated | Done before; mostly repeatable | Done rarely; limited precedent | First time for DTS or industry; no template |
| Reputation & stakeholder risk | Internal; low visibility | Cross-team; moderate visibility | Executive-visible or customer-facing | Board/regulator/public exposure; high stakes |
| Technology maturity | Mature, well-established tech | Established with some new elements | Substantially new technology | Emerging/unproven; key tech not yet in use |
| Business change impact | No process/behaviour change | Minor change; few users | Significant change; whole team/function | Major change across multiple business units |

### Step-by-step process
1. Extract and summarise all slide content relevant to scope, technology,
   stakeholders, cost, timeline, and organisational impact.
2. Score each of the six dimensions 1–4 against the rubric, citing the deck
   evidence used.
3. Sum the total (max 24).
4. Apply the dollar gate (Section 6.3) if cost and discipline are stated —
   the gate can only escalate, never de-escalate, the score-derived tier.
5. Apply the severe-risk override: if Execution risk = 4, Reputation risk = 4,
   or (for regulated/novel-tech work) Technology maturity = 4, escalate one
   tier automatically regardless of total score.
6. Map the (possibly escalated) total to a tier using Section 6.4:
   - Minor Works: 6–8
   - Small Projects: 9–12
   - Standard: 13–17
   - Major: 18–24
7. State any information gaps that limited confidence in the scoring, and
   recommend what the presenter should add to the deck to firm up the
   assessment.

## Output format (mandatory)

Produce output in this exact structure — no additional preamble:

**Project name:** [from deck, or "Not stated"]

| Dimension | Score (1–4) | Commentary |
|---|---|---|
| Execution risk | X | One or two sentences, evidence-based |
| Complexity | X | One or two sentences, evidence-based |
| Familiarity & repeatability | X | One or two sentences, evidence-based |
| Reputation & stakeholder risk | X | One or two sentences, evidence-based |
| Technology maturity | X | One or two sentences, evidence-based |
| Business change impact | X | One or two sentences, evidence-based |
| **Total** | **XX / 24** | |

**Dollar gate applied:** [Discipline / band / effect, or "Not stated in deck — gate not applied"]

**Severe-risk override:** [Triggered / Not triggered — reason]

**Resulting governance tier:** [Minor Works / Small Projects / Standard / Major]

**Confidence & gaps:** Brief note on what wasn't covered in the deck and would
sharpen the assessment (e.g. cost, stakeholder list, technology stack).

## Principles to follow
- Be objective and evidence-based — score what is in the deck, not what is
  assumed about the project type.
- Keep commentary concise (one to two sentences per dimension).
- Never let cost lower a score-derived tier — dollar gate is escalate-only.
- Flag missing information rather than filling gaps with speculation.
- Do not recommend artefacts/governance detail beyond the tier — that is a
  separate exercise (Section 7 of the framework), out of scope for this skill
  unless the user explicitly asks for it as a follow-up.